lesson reading-suffixes v=1 format=teach grade=4 unit=2 subject=reading levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Suffixes"
  goal "Read a word with a suffix, say how the suffix changes the root word's meaning and job, and spell it."

  look {
    scene 35x9 {
      row all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=end {
        dog d facing=1 ball=1
        wordbricks a bricks=["play", "ful"] root=0 roots=1 blank=-1 word="playful" labels=true
        wordbricks b bricks=["care", "less"] root=0 roots=1 blank=-1 word="careless" labels=true
      }
    }
    say """
      A suffix is a small part at the end of a word. It changes what the word means, and it can
      change the job the word does. Ful means full of, so a playful dog is full of play, and less
      means without, so careless means without care. Ness turns a describing word into a thing,
      and er can mean a person who does something.

      Some roots change their spelling when a suffix goes on: happy and ness make happiness.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked reading.suffix-meaning k=4 level=medium
    }
    practice reading.suffix-meaning count=2 seed=3 hard-seed=7
    practice reading.suffix-build count=1 seed=2
    practice reading.suffix-match count=1 seed=2
    practice reading.suffix-in-sentence count=2 seed=4 hard-seed=10
    practice reading.suffix-spelling count=2 seed=5 hard-seed=7
    practice reading.er-not-a-person count=1 hard-count=0 seed=3
    level hard {
      practice reading.suffix-used-right count=1 seed=7
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show reading.suffix-used-right k=0 hard-k=3
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show reading.longest-word k=0
  }
  remember {
    say "A suffix goes at the end. It can change the meaning, the job and sometimes the spelling."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Collect words with one root and different endings: help, helper, helpful, helpless, helping.
    Seeing the same root on every brick is what makes a long new word readable, and knowing what
    the ending does often gives its meaning without a dictionary. In the three-star question a
    word can take two suffixes, one after the other; let the child try the bricks in different
    orders and say each one aloud to hear whether it is a real word.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level enjoyment is worked once in the teacher's pen before careful, fearless and teacher, the suffix to build with and the matches are as written, the gaps want builder, harmless, careful and playful, the bricks make happiness, plentiful and hopeful, the er that is not a person is as written, and the two-star and three-star are as written."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to read are kindness, teacher and enjoyment, the suffix to build with and the matches are as written, the gaps want kindness, harmless and darkness, the bricks make beautiful, laziness and penniless, whose y turns to i, the er question makes way for a second sentence to judge, helpful, the two-star's word is hopeless, and the three-star is as written."
  }
}
